@h1 Install the packages h1@

Wit ships as five small ESM packages with a strict dependency direction.
Installing the wrong subset — or reaching for CommonJS @code require code@ — is
the first thing that goes wrong. This page gets the packages into a project and
confirms they import.

@h2 Prerequisites h2@

@ul
@li @strong Node ≥ 20. strong@ Verified on v22. li@
@li @strong ESM only. strong@ Every package is @code "type": "module" code@.
There is no CommonJS build; a @@@code require('@witlang/parser') code@@@ throws.
Use @code import code@, or dynamic @code import() code@ from a CommonJS file. li@
ul@

@h2 The package map h2@

Renderers and the CLI depend on the runtime; the runtime depends on the parser.
The arrows point from dependent to dependency:

@@@pre
@witlang/render-html  ─┐
@witlang/render-markdown ┼─▶ @witlang/runtime ─▶ @witlang/parser
@witlang/cli          ─┘
pre@@@

Import @strong only from each package's entry point. strong@ Everything under
@code dist/ code@ beyond the package root is package-private and may move
between releases.

@h2 Minimal install h2@

The common case is the parser, the runtime, and one renderer:

@@@pre
npm i @witlang/parser @witlang/runtime @witlang/render-html
pre@@@

Add @@@code @witlang/render-markdown code@@@ if you also want Markdown output.
You rarely install the parser or runtime alone — a renderer pulls the runtime,
which pulls the parser.

@h2 The CLI h2@

Install @@@code @witlang/cli code@@@ for the @code wit code@ binary. It is the
only package with a @code bin code@, and it bundles its own copies of the
pipeline, so it works standalone — you do not need the library packages
installed to run @code wit code@.

@@@pre
npm i -g @witlang/cli     # global `wit` on your PATH
npm i -D @witlang/cli     # or per-project, run via npx wit
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 @code wit --version code@ prints @code 0.2.0 code@, the
@code VERSION code@ constant in the CLI, matching the published packages. aside@

@h2 Smoke test h2@

Four lines that parse and render prose confirm the whole chain imports:

@@@pre
import { parse } from '@witlang/parser';
import { resolve, expand } from '@witlang/runtime';
import { renderHtml } from '@witlang/render-html';

console.log(renderHtml(expand(resolve(parse('Hello *there*.')))));
// → <article class="wit-doc"><p>Hello <strong>there</strong>.</p></article>
pre@@@

From the CLI, the equivalent is a one-liner against a file:

@@@pre
wit build hello.wit        # styled HTML document on stdout
pre@@@

@h2 TypeScript h2@

Types ship with every package; there is nothing to install separately. Import
type-only symbols from the entry point, never from an internal path:

@@@pre
import type { Document, NodeUse } from '@witlang/parser';
import type { ExpandedDocument, ResolveOptions } from '@witlang/runtime';
import type { RenderHtmlOptions } from '@witlang/render-html';
pre@@@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Deep-importing a private module such as
@@@code @witlang/runtime/dist/expander.js code@@@. It is package-private and
may move — import @@@code resolve code@@@, @@@code expand code@@@ from the
package root instead. li@
@li Expecting CommonJS. Mixing a CommonJS build tool without ESM interop breaks
the import; the packages are ESM-only. li@
@li Installing @@@code @witlang/render-html code@@@ but calling @code resolve code@
without having @@@code @witlang/runtime code@@@ resolvable — the renderer
depends on it, so npm will pull it, but a hand-managed @code node_modules code@
can miss it. li@
ul@

